From a30b49aeca58db15e0064ea2cae0ed8bf0e8d250 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Manoj Sekar <sekar@qti.qualcomm.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 12:53:43 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] Channel Planning: Enhance rank caching with keying of
 (opclass, chan, bw)

The CBS preference-table cache (AgentDB::sRadio::cbs_preference_table.
by_opclass_channel) was a 2-level map keyed on (op_class, chan). For 6GHz
op-class 137 (320MHz), a single (op_class, chan) pair corresponds to TWO
physically distinct wide channel blocks (BANDWIDTH_320_1 and
BANDWIDTH_320_2) with different center frequencies, different beacon-
channel coverage and different driver ranks. Because both variants map
to the SAME op-class number, refresh_preference_table()'s
new_cache[op_class][chan] = entry write collapsed both into ONE cell,
so whichever the supported_bw_list iterated LAST silently overwrote the
other. On the current QCA driver ordering that put 320MHz-1 last with
rank=0, silently dropping the positive rank of the 320MHz-2 block the
driver had actually scanned. select_best_channel_from_cbs() then saw
NO positively-ranked (>0) channel in the controller-preferred set and
fell back to the cumulative-preference score, defeating CBS-based
channel selection on any 6GHz 320MHz deployment.

Reproduced on a 6GHz-only channel-selection request with preferred set
P={61,65,...,157} and driver-ranked 320MHz-2 blocks at primary channels
{37, 85, 117, 149, 165} (rank 4, 3, 7, 1, 2 respectively). Cache dump
before fix showed every op_class=137 entry as bw=320MHz-1 rank=0, and
the log line "NO positively-ranked (>0) channel in the controller-
preferred set for radio ... -> CBS has no useful ordering" fired on
every request.

Fix: make the cache bandwidth-variant-aware by adding bw as a third map
key, so both 320MHz-1 and 320MHz-2 for the same (op_class, chan) are
stored independently. The two variants then compete against each other
in select_best_channel_from_cbs() via the existing "lowest rank_key
wins, tie-break by wider bw" policy. Because the driver only ranks the
block it physically scanned, exactly one variant carries a positive
rank and the other stays at rank=0 -> the positive-ranked variant wins
its (op_class, chan) cell deterministically and is picked as the CBS
winner. For every other op-class the inner map has a single entry per
(op_class, chan), so behaviour is byte-for-byte identical to before.

Every other layer that touches this data was already variant-safe (the
fronthaul HAL bw_info_list[bw], the CHANNELS_LIST_RESPONSE transport
and the outgoing Channel Preference Report all key on the specific
BANDWIDTH_320_1 / BANDWIDTH_320_2), so this change is scoped to the
backhaul CBS cache and its readers.

Changes:

  agent/src/beerocks/slave/agent_db.h
    - sPreferenceTable::by_opclass_channel: change the value type from
      map<uint8_t, sPreferenceEntry> to
      map<uint8_t, map<eWiFiBandwidth, sPreferenceEntry>> so each
      (op_class, chan) can hold both 320MHz-1 and 320MHz-2 entries
      side by side. Added a block comment explaining the collision and
      why the extra dimension is required.

  agent/src/beerocks/slave/tasks/channel_selection_task.cpp
    - refresh_preference_table():
      - new_cache type upgraded to the 3-level shape.
      - Writer projects the rank into new_cache[op_class][chan][bw]
        so both 320MHz variants coexist.
      - Overlay post-loop (DFS UNAVAILABLE / AWGN / bw-not-supported)
        adds the extra bw-level iteration so overlays are applied to
        every (op_class, chan, bw) entry independently.
      - SUCCESS cache dump log iterates the bw-level too; total_entries
        now counts (op_class, chan, bw) rows.
    - select_best_channel_from_cbs():
      - "any positive rank in preferred" pre-check iterates the bw
        level too so the positive-ranked variant is visible even when
        its sibling variant carries rank=0.
      - Winner-selection loop iterates the bw level; the existing
        rank_key comparison + wider-bw tie-break naturally selects the
        positively-ranked variant against its rank=0 sibling.

Regression scope:
  6GHz op-class 137 (320MHz): both variants now preserved and reachable.
  6GHz op-class 131/132/133/134: single-variant per (chan) -> no change.
  5GHz op-class 128/129/130 (80/160MHz), op-class 116/117/119/120/125/
    126/127 (40MHz), op-class 115/118/121/124 (20MHz): single-variant
    per (op_class, chan) -> inner map has 1 entry -> byte-for-byte
    identical cache content and identical selection outcome as before.
  2.4GHz op-class 81/83/84: same, single-variant -> unchanged.
  Fronthaul HAL, CHANNELS_LIST_RESPONSE transport, Channel Preference
    Report TLV builder, legacy select_next_channel and ZWDFS
    select_best_usable_channel: unchanged (all already variant-aware).

Verified on 6GHz 320MHz test: cache now shows both variants per (137,
chan) with rank=4/3/7/1/2 preserved on the 320MHz-2 rows, and the CBS
selection winner correctly resolves to chan=149 bw=320MHz-2 rank=1 ->
real channel switch (no cumulative-score fallback).

Additionally this change also update the base interface tracking for
setting blocklist appropriately.

Signed-off-by: Manoj Sekar <sekar@qti.qualcomm.com>
